# Complete visual regression coverage

## Scope and existing evidence

Compare semantic layout, vector topology, layer composition, final pixels,
digital themes, live themes, and overflow overlays independently.

Current executable evidence now requires one independently computed result for
semantic layout, vector topology, layer composition, render seeds, physical
bounds, final pixels, digital theme, live theme, and overflow overlays before a
render-regression report is structurally complete. Duplicate or missing axes
reject, while mismatches remain explicit per axis instead of being hidden by a
passing final-pixel result. Leave-one-out and
one-duplicate-at-a-time matrices prove every axis independently participates in
both completeness checks. Axis-specific comparators, pixel tolerances, golden
artifact storage, renderer output, and visual acceptance policy remain open.

Reversed all-mismatch evidence also proves every axis is surfaced in canonical
mismatch order. Complete reports now return constructor-sealed evidence
borrowing the exact caller sequence, and canonical mismatch projection can run
from that admitted view without repeating structural interpretation.

A 512-state exhaustive match/mismatch oracle covers every combination of the
nine axes, reverses caller evidence order, and requires the exact direct and
sealed canonical mismatch projection for each mask. Missing- and duplicate-axis
fixtures also require direct/sealed error parity.

## Finite two-viewport golden-artifact contract

**Inputs:** one accepted bilingual-notebook representative A4 page with
source-owned text, a small two-column table, math and a line-art shape;
fixed render seed, chosen font/glyph profiles and an immutable semantic
revision. Use exactly two digital preview widths, `320 px` and `1024 px`,
with corresponding paper and live single-pen theme declarations.
**Outputs:** 18 axis/viewport comparison records, not a single visually
plausible screenshot. No golden pixels are claimed to exist today.

- **Semantic layout**
  - **Candidate golden:** Element IDs, order and rectangles
  - **Proposed comparison:** Exact IDs/order; measured boxes within 0.25 mm
- **Vector topology**
  - **Candidate golden:** Paths, joins, pen-contact kinds
  - **Proposed comparison:** Exact path count, endpoint and adjacency graph
- **Layer composition**
  - **Candidate golden:** Ordered material/layer owner list
  - **Proposed comparison:** Exact layering and clipping policy
- **Render seed**
  - **Candidate golden:** Stable seed and semantic revision
  - **Proposed comparison:** Exact identity
- **Physical bounds**
  - **Candidate golden:** Page, safety inset and overflow
  - **Proposed comparison:** Exact declared bounds; 0 out-of-page marks
- **Final pixels**
  - **Candidate golden:** Reference PNG for each width
  - **Proposed comparison:** At most 1% pixels exceed 1/255 channel error
- **Digital theme**
  - **Candidate golden:** Foreground/background tokens
  - **Proposed comparison:** Exact role tokens and minimum contrast policy
- **Live theme**
  - **Candidate golden:** Pen ink, stroke and blank-sheet model
  - **Proposed comparison:** Exact selected tool, contact and geometry
- **Overflow overlays**
  - **Candidate golden:** Owner IDs, issue shape and count
  - **Proposed comparison:** Exact issue count/owner, 0 missed overflow


**Golden inventory:** `page-a4-320` and `page-a4-1024`, each with one
semantic JSON representation, vector trace, layer snapshot, render seed,
physical-bounds record, expected PNG, digital-theme policy, live-theme
policy, and overflow record. This means two reference page bundles and
nine independently evaluated axes per width. Any *actual* pixel reference
must come from a reviewed renderer output and be fixed to a versioned
renderer/font environment, not generated from the candidate under test.

**Example failure:** a 1024-pixel screenshot matches its reference while
its underlying vector paths join two originally separate pen strokes. The
pixel axis can pass but the vector axis must fail; the complete result
cannot pass. A 320-pixel overflow marker missing for the same source block
fails its own overlay axis even if the semantic order is unchanged.

**Admission and risk:** the current Rust structural evidence reports all
nine axes independently, but the specified thresholds and golden bundles
are only an acceptance *proposal*. No current renderer comparison, golden
PNG files or physical plot was generated here. One missing axis, stale
seed, unsupported rendering capability, or unavailable source golden
produces `IncompleteRegressionEvidence`, never a fabricated pass.

## Acceptance decision

- **Basis:** theoretical nine-axis golden plan, 2026-11-02; existing typed
  completeness and axis-order tests do not establish visual correctness.
- **Delivered:** representative page, two widths, 18 comparisons, inventory,
  axis-specific tolerances and missing-golden refusal.
- **Unavailable:** golden pixel fixtures, real render comparators, calibrated
  visual thresholds, screenshot approvals or physical color assessment.
